TYPESCRIPT - RECAP NOTES<br><br>1. WHAT IS TYPESCRIPT?<br>- TypeScript = JavaScript + static type checking.<br>- It catches many errors before the code runs.<br>- Browsers and Node.js run JavaScript, so TypeScript must be compiled.<br><br>Install:<br>npm install -g typescript<br><br>Compile and run:<br>tsc basics.ts<br>node basics.js<br><br>Watch mode (automatically recompiles on changes):<br>tsc basics.ts --watch<br><br><br>2. BASIC TYPES<br><br>let userName: string = "Kiran";<br>let age: number = 25;<br>let isActive: boolean = true;<br><br>Other useful types:<br>let emptyValue: null = null;<br>let notSet: undefined = undefined;<br><br>Use lowercase types:<br>string, number, boolean<br>Do not use String, Number, Boolean for normal type annotations.<br><br><br>3. TYPE INFERENCE<br><br>TypeScript can infer a type from the first assigned value:<br><br>let score = 10;       // number<br>let message = "Hi";   // string<br><br>score = 20;           // valid<br>// score = "twenty";  // error<br><br>Use explicit types when the value may not be clear initially:<br>let city: string;<br>city = "Bengaluru";<br><br><br>4. ANY, UNKNOWN, AND NEVER<br><br>any:<br>let value: any = 10;<br>value = "hello"; // allowed<br>- Avoid any when possible: it removes type safety.<br><br>unknown:<br>let input: unknown = "hello";<br>if (typeof input === "string") {<br>  console.log(input.toUpperCase());<br>}<br>- Safer than any because it must be checked before use.<br><br>never:<br>function throwError(message: string): never {<br>  throw new Error(message);<br>}<br>- Used when a function never finishes normally.<br><b>5. FUNCTIONS<br></b>function add(a: number, b: number): number {<br>  return a + b;<br>}<br><br><b><i>Arrow function:<br></i></b>const multiply = (a: number, b: number): number =&gt; a * b;<br><br>Void function (does not return a value):<br>function printMessage(message: string): void {<br>  console.log(message);<br>}<br><br><b><i>Optional parameter:<br></i></b>function greet(name: string, title?: string): string {<br>  return title ? `Hello ${title} ${name}` : `Hello ${name}`;<br>}<br><i><b>Default parameter:<br></b></i>function addWithDefault(a: number, b: number = 5): number {<br>  return a + b;<br>}<br>addWithDefault(10); // 15<br><br><b>6. ARRAYS<br></b>------------<br>let hobbies: string[] = ["sports", "cooking"];<br>hobbies.push("reading");<br><br>// hobbies.push(10); // error<br>Alternative syntax:<br>let scores: Array&lt;number&gt; = [10, 20, 30];<br><br>Mixed-value array:<br>let values: (string | number)[] = ["abc", 10, "xyz"];<br><br><b>7. UNION TYPES</b><br>A union allows more than one type:<br><br>let age: string | number = 36;<br>age = "thirty-six";<br><br>Useful with functions:<br>function printId(id: string | number): void {<br>  console.log(id);<br>}<br><b>8. TUPLES</b><br>A tuple has a fixed number of items and types:<br><br><div>let userInfo: [string, number] = ["Kiran", 25];<br>let coordinates: [number, number] = [12.97, 77.59];<br>// userInfo = [25, "Kiran"]; // error<br><br><b>9. OBJECT TYPES</b><br>let user: {<br>  name: string;<br>  age: number | string;<br>  hobbies: string[];<br>  role: { desc: string; id: number };<br>} = {<br>  name: "Max",<br>  age: 28,<br>  hobbies: ["sports", "cooking"],<br>  role: { desc: "Admin", id: 5 }<br>};<br><br>Optional object property:<br>let profile: { name: string; email?: string } = {<br>  name: "Kiran"<br>};<br><br><b>10. TYPE ALIAS</b><br>Use type aliases to avoid repeating long object types:<br>type User = {<br>  name: string;<br>  age: number;<br>  hobbies: string[];<br>};<br>let user1: User = {<br>  name: "Kiran",<br>  age: 25,<br>  hobbies: ["coding"]<br>};<br><b>11. RECORD</b><br>Record is useful for objects with flexible keys:</div><div><br>let data: Record&lt;string, string | number&gt; = {<br>  id: 1,<br>  name: "Kiran",<br>  city: "Bengaluru"<br>};<br><br>Meaning:<br>Record&lt;keyType, valueType&gt;</div><div><br><b>12. ENUMS </b></div><div>only available for Type SCript<b><br></b>enum Role {<br>  Admin,  // 0<br>  Editor, // 1<br>  Guest   // 2<br>}<br>let userRole: Role = 0; // 0 is assigned</div><div>let userRole: Role = Role.Editor; // 1 is assigned<br>let userRole: Role = Role.Admin; //0 is assigned<br><br><b>Custom numeric values:<br></b>enum RoleCode {<br>  Admin = 5,<br>  Editor = 7,<br>  Guest = 8<br>}<br><b>string enum:<br></b>enum Status {<br>  Active = "ACTIVE",<br>  Inactive = "INACTIVE"<br>}<br><br><b>13. STRING LITERAL UNION</b><br>Often preferred over enums for a small fixed set of values:<br><br></div><div>type UserRole = "admin" | "editor" | "guest";<br>let role: UserRole = "admin";<br>// role = "manager"; // error</div><div><b>14. TYPE ALIAS / CUSTOM TYPE
</b>Creates a reusable name for a type.</div><div><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:var(--thread-content-margin-xs,calc(var(--spacing)*4))] @w-sm/main:[--thread-content-margin:var(--thread-content-margin-sm,calc(var(--spacing)*6))] @w-lg/main:[--thread-content-margin:var(--thread-content-margin-lg,calc(var(--spacing)*16))] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div data-conversation-screenshot-content="" class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @w-lg/main:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden @[53.5rem]/main:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col gap-4 grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="b08e7747-c649-58cc-9405-0b77cd1da26c" dir="auto" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5.6-terra-wm" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal outline-none keyboard-focused:focus-ring [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert wrap-break-word w-full light markdown-new-styling"><pre class="overflow-visible! px-0!" data-start="0" data-end="305" data-is-last-node=""><div class="relative w-full mt-4 mb-1"><div class=""><div class="contents"><div class="relative"><div class="h-full min-h-0 min-w-0"><div class="h-full min-h-0 min-w-0"><div class="border border-token-border-light border-radius-3xl corner-superellipse/1.1 rounded-3xl"><div class="h-full w-full border-radius-3xl bg-(--code-block-surface) corner-superellipse/1.1 overflow-clip rounded-3xl [--code-block-surface:var(--bg-elevated-secondary)] dark:[--code-block-surface:var(--composer-surface-primary)] lxnfua_clipPathFallback"><div class="relative"><div class="pe-11 pt-3"><div class="relative z-0 flex max-w-full"><div id="code-block-viewer" dir="ltr" class="q9tKkq_viewer cm-editor z-10 light:cm-light dark:cm-light flex h-full w-full flex-col items-stretch ͼd ͼr"><div class="cm-scroller"><pre class="cm-content q9tKkq_readonly m-0 PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer"><code>Example:
type Roles = 'admin' | 'editor'| 'guest';
function rolef(r: Roles){
  console.log(r);
}<br>rolef('admin'); //works<br>rolef('zxcv'); //doesn't work<br>type User = {name: String;<br>             age: number;<br>             role: Roles;<br>             hobbies: string[]}
let user: User = {
  name: "Kiran",
  age: 25,<br>  role: 'admin' // works <br>  role: 'superadmin' //doesn't work
  hobbies: ["coding", "reading"]
};<br><b>15.<span class="ͼe">Function return type</span></b>
<span class="ͼg">function</span> <span class="ͼm">add</span>(<span class="ͼm">a</span>: <span class="ͼm">number</span>, <span class="ͼm">b</span>: <span class="ͼm">number</span>): <span class="ͼm">number</span> {
  <span class="ͼg">return</span> <span class="ͼm">a</span> <span class="ͼg">+</span> <span class="ͼm">b</span>;
}
<span class="ͼg">function</span> <span class="ͼm">greet</span>(<span class="ͼm">name</span>: <span class="ͼm">string</span>): <span class="ͼm">string</span> {
  <span class="ͼg">return</span> <span class="ͼk">`Hello, </span>${<span class="ͼm">name</span>}<span class="ͼk">`</span>;
}
<span class="ͼe">// void = function returns nothing</span>
<span class="ͼg">function</span> <span class="ͼm">printMessage</span>(<span class="ͼm">message</span>: <span class="ͼm">string</span>): <span class="ͼm">v</span><span class="ͼg">oid</span> {
  <span class="ͼm">console</span><span class="ͼg">.</span>log(<span class="ͼm">message</span>);
}<br><br><br>14. QUICK RULES
</code></pre></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></pre></div></div></div></div></div></div><br>- Prefer specific types instead of any.<br>- Use union types when a value can have different types.<br>- Use arrays for lists of similar values.<br>- Use tuples for fixed-position values.<br>- Use type aliases for reusable object structures.<br>- Use Record for dynamic key-value objects.<br>- Use string literal unions for a small fixed set of choices.<br>- TypeScript types disappear after compilation; runtime code is JavaScript.</div>
